2186153293 #edb/ref 1061 601718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153293 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38e288bc-7c3f-536d-a984-63e199038d6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153293 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153294 #edb/ref 1061 601721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4499c456-0b30-59ea-92cd-0a6e8cc58d01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153295 #edb/ref 1061 601722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153295 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2779fb81-53c1-5556-969d-409a83b9a273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153295 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153335 #edb/ref 1064 "Similar to first-order linear ODEs, for inhomogeneous differential equations, the **general solution** is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the **complementary solution** (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- $y_p(x)$ is a **particular solution** of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWe will apply this structure to a specific differential equation in the next slide." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153335 #edb/ref 1065 14780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153335 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d323d4d9-40c7-5a00-aafe-a0c1ba46fd4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153335 #edb/ref 1067 "General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153382 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the second-order linear homogeneous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' - 5y' + 6y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that a fundamental set of solutions is given by\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = e^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe superposition principle tells us that any linear combination of solutions to a linear, homogeneous differential equation is also a solution to the equation. Therefore, the **general solution** to this differential equation is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{3x} + Be^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are **arbitrary constants.**\n\nIn general, if an $n$th order homogeneous, linear ODE has a fundamental set of solutions given by \n\n$$\ny_1(x), \\quad y_2(x),\\quad \\ldots, \\quad y_n(x),\n$$\n\nthen the general solution to the equation is \n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y(x) = C_1y_1(x)+C_2y_2(x)+\\cdots+C_ny_n(x)}\n$$\n\nfor arbitrary constants $C_1, C_2,\\ldots C_n.$\n\n**Note:** Using sigma notation, we can write the general solution more compactly as\n\n$$\ny(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n C_i y_i(x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153382 #edb/ref 1065 4197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153382 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d0ae06ba-917f-56b1-8d5d-fac54f464ee8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153382 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263134 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263134 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263134 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f1c568d-36bc-51c0-84f0-502d1edec67c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263204 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263204 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263204 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15c6b34c-c545-5d3c-acb9-3150c86a0408" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263907 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263907 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263907 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6991653b-499f-5aa9-839d-82c296e784eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264404 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264404 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264404 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a4b70ef3-acdc-5304-9dab-af64de3228a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265154 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{4x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265154 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265154 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f636dd7a-29d2-590e-895b-f51e6bc5bb9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265577 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265577 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265577 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265577 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265577 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186265154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265577 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265577 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265577 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186263907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265577 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "becc03c6-febe-52bf-9a33-abc159de7662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265788 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265788 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129942" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265788 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265788 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265788 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "edcd3710-0bbf-55f6-957d-428926b6e84b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265788 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})-2({u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}-2u{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{2x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354772 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{3}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354772 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354772 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bf2cf10-889a-5e2c-a4c9-c7d6aadbce53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356170 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356170 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356170 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aa16421b-994d-5da6-863d-6ea3a79896bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356356 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356356 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356356 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c14821fb-4141-5115-9012-5b4f4293ea36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356706 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{x}^{3}+Bx" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356706 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356706 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e9843801-abf9-5cf7-a76d-830f177eef7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356721 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356721 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356721 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eba3f05e-2836-5879-ae2f-d7c77543ae46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356915 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186354772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356915 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356915 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356915 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356915 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356915 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356915 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356915 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186356170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356915 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "02d6a845-e776-58ed-a2a7-d5b5b0fd2a6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357476 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357476 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357476 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357476 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25f23664-d443-5442-98c5-f5ec1e9dae76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357476 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-6(u{x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-6u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx{w}^{′}+6w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-5$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-5\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380277 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186380278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380277 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380277 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380277 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380277 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e072db4c-ae23-5874-8e05-5e0e80f81647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380277 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=2w\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=1\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380278 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380278 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380278 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186380279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380278 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe6acf30-551e-5b2f-95cc-84791c4dfae6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380279 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380279 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380279 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "67952861-f148-5199-901d-802ad7eb